> To save the knowledge somewhere that the
> bones saver can access it is and is not trivial.

It would break backward compatibility, but apart
from that it fits every definition of "trivial" (that
is relevant to computer programming) with which
I am familiar.

> One can add global variables quite easily,

This wouldn't be global, because it would be
different for every level.  So, obviously, it would
be added to the data structure used for storing
(the current state of) a level.

> but that's not good programing style as
> sooner or later the global variables start
> to hinder the normal namespace.

Namespace issues in general are easily solved
by being slightly more verbose with variable
names, but in this case there's no reason for
the new variable to be global.  Logically, it
should be part of the data structure for the
level.  I'm not surprised it wasn't originally
included, because of Hack's age and the
limits on system resources back then, but if
the code were being written today, or ten
years ago for that matter, I would certainly
expect this kind of information to be included
in the level data structure.  Thus, I don't see
any problem with adding it.  Heck, while
we're at it, let's go ahead and add level title
and author fields, which the UI can optionally
make available to the user or not.  All of that
combined will take up quite a bit less memory
(per level) than the headers on this message.

For a full dungeon with seventy levels or so,
putting definition-file path and filename plus
level title and author on every level that's
been generated, I'm suggesting adding to
the saved game file less than one sector's
worth of data.  The cost of that extra storage
capacity, in this era of $60 terabyte hard
drives, is effectively zero, even if you're
running a major public server.  (How many
saved games does NAO have on it right
now?  Fifty thousand, maybe?  However
many there are, the space they take up
is negligible compared to all the ttyrecs.)


